<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aga of Kish</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Agga of Kish</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aga of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> was a king of Kish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>, around the twenty-seventh century BCE, listed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> as the last ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of Kish and the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmebaragesi">Enmebaragesi</a>. He is known chiefly from the Sumerian poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> and Aga, which tells how he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and was captured and then released by Gilgamesh.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aga-of-kish.jpg" alt="Aga of Kish" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Metropolitan Museum of Art · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aga_of_Kish_Stele_of_Ushumgal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aga, written Agga or Akka in some sources, was king in the first dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> in the Early Dynastic I period, around 2700 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> counts him as the 23rd king of Kish and many sources name him as son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmebaragesi">Enmebaragesi</a>. His name is Sumerian and rarely attested in early texts, which makes him easy to spot. It probably means made by a god. He appears in the Stele of Ushumgal, where he is a Great Assembly official, and in the Tummal Inscription, which records that he made the Tummal pre-eminent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> held the hegemony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and that Aga reigned 625 years, a figure that cannot be taken literally, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> defeated him. His rule probably covered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> and Zabala, a dependency of Umma; he appears as king of Umma in the Gem of King Aga. There is some scant evidence that the rulers of Kish, like the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> kings, courted the authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> to legitimise a claim to leadership over Sumer or part of it. Archaeology shows Kish flourishing in the Early Dynastic II period, with influence beyond its own territory, and declining rapidly in the Early Dynastic III period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Sumerian poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> and Aga tells how Aga, treating Gilgamesh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, demanded labour to finish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and irrigation works at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>. Gilgamesh put the demand before the city fathers, who refused, and then to the able-bodied men, who declared him lugal. After ten days Aga besieged Uruk. Birhurtura, Gilgamesh's guard, went out, was captured and tortured. When Gilgamesh looked over the wall his radiance terrified the Kishite army, and Enkidu and the men of Uruk captured Aga in the midst of it. Gilgamesh addressed him as his superior, recalling Aga's past help, and set him free to return to Kish.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aga of Kish” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sumerian poems tell of Gilgamesh resisting <b>Aga of Kish</b>, and of Enmerkar demanding precious stone from the distant land of Aratta; these are literary works composed centuries later, and how far they reflect real events is uncertain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Thorkild Jacobsen argued in 1943 that assemblies had once decided war and peace, citing the Sumerian poem of Gilgamesh and <b>Aga of Kish</b>, in which Gilgamesh consults the elders and then the young men of Uruk before defying Kish.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
